docs/implementation/2026-11-14-adjustments-repair.md:13:3. Temperature: multiply red by `2 ** (.5*a)`, blue by `2 ** (-.5*a)`, green unchanged, then clamp. Positive warms, negative cools. This is an explicit channel-gain approximation to white balance, with no sepia or hue rotation; it is not calibrated Kelvin/CCT or chromatic adaptation.
docs/implementation/2026-11-14-adjustments-repair.md:16:6. Clarity (`sharpness` parameter): finite Gaussian low pass at sigma 6 document pixels (25×25 support); `clamp(original + .8*a*(original-lowpass))`. Positive strengthens local edges, negative softens; flat fields retain brightness. The broader lowpass produces local contrast across a 12-pixel radius rather than narrow sigma-1/radius-2 edge sharpening. It is a fixed-scale local contrast approximation, not an adaptive or multiscale photographic clarity model.
docs/implementation/2026-11-14-adjustments-repair.md:19:Convolution samples outside image/filter input as transparent (`edgeMode=none`), weights each color by sample alpha, normalizes by accumulated alpha, and restores original alpha. SVG performs premultiplied convolution (`preserveAlpha=false`), makes its unpremultiplied result opaque, then composites it into SourceAlpha. This avoids dark RGB halos from empty transparent neighbors. Filter region pads the document by 24 pixels (maximum composed kernel support is eighteen); final SVG document clipping remains authoritative. Filters run in document coordinates after the layer transform; CPU fixtures are untransformed pixel arrays with one array pixel per document pixel.
docs/implementation/2026-11-14-adjustments-repair.md:45:Pure pixel fixtures verify the CPU algorithms; DOM tests verify emitted SVG primitives, parameter sharing and export preservation. They do **not** prove native browser/WebKit bitmap equality. SVG engines may introduce intermediate quantization, premultiplication rounding, interpolation/resampling, or implementation-specific convolution behavior. No numeric native tolerance is asserted without an observed native raster comparison. Independent acceptance should rasterize fixed color/alpha cards through the production browser or Tauri/WebKit export path and compare decoded PNG pixels with the CPU reference, recording engine/version and measured tolerance. Source image color management, transformed/antialiased content, document scaling and large-kernel performance need native visual coverage. Parameter histories remain non-destructive; no commit/push/API requests were made by this repair worker.
docs/implementation/2026-11-14-adjustments-repair.md:51:Independent reviewer reported CPU reference timings of approximately 1115ms for 128² and 4966ms for 256² images with spatial operations. These timings were supplied by the reviewer rather than reproduced in this worker. The CPU reference is deliberately test/verification code and is not the interactive canvas path. Production SVG still uses finite 25×25 clarity plus up to 13×13 blur kernels; native rendering cost may be significant. Native performance, interactive smoothness, and browser/WebKit raster pixel equality remain pending acceptance evidence. No native performance or equivalence completion claim is made.
docs/implementation/2026-11-14-adjustments-repair.md:53:## Actual browser failure, diagnosis, and separable repair
docs/implementation/2026-11-14-adjustments-repair.md:57:The reviewer then operated the standalone diagnostic HTML through UI and downloaded four PNGs. Offline comparison is recorded with file SHA256 hashes and pixel samples in `fixtures/adjustment-native-convolution-diagnosis.json`. **Dense sigma6/order25×25 matches identity in all 16384 RGBA bytes (max0, mean0, nonzero0)**. Equivalent separable25×1→1×25 differs from identity in 11187 bytes, max122/mean19.1187. Order13×13 also works. Actual production SVG properties and normalized weights are correct, ruling out missing attributes/serialization. This establishes that this browser's large dense convolution path does not apply the intended operation; the engine's exact internal cutoff or fallback mechanism is not inferred from this test.
docs/implementation/2026-11-14-adjustments-repair.md:59:Repair uses normalized 1-D Gaussian kernels along horizontal then vertical axes. Premultiplied colors and convolved alpha pass through both axes without restoring coverage or normalizing between them; normalization and original-alpha restoration happen after the complete convolution. The CPU reference uses the same separable mathematics and retains the dense outer-product helper for reference/diagnostics. Every one of the eleven 64px reference cards is byte-identical before and after this CPU optimization. An additional independently implemented dense alpha-weighted test checks a partial-alpha card within one byte, while exact plateau expectations 174/146 remain unchanged. New red SVG regression expected orders25×1/1×25 but observed25×25 before repair; after repair 8 files/46 tests passed, actual exit0. Production build actual exit0 with the existing bundle-size advisory.
docs/implementation/2026-11-14-adjustments-repair.md:61:A fresh local CPU-only run at clarity70+blur100 measured 19.697ms for128² and46.995ms for256², recorded in `fixtures/adjustment-reference-timings.json`; benchmark artifact test passed exit0. These single-run CPU numbers do not establish native interactive performance. Production browser re-export after this repair remains necessary, and Tauri/WebKit pixel/performance checks remain outstanding. The main reviewer is preparing a new static build for that fresh browser acceptance; old pre-repair PNGs cannot establish success.
